Exactly. In the familiar elephant-stake metaphor, the constraint can survive long after the thing that originally made it binding has disappeared.

And your addition is better:

You can even carry the old stake around as jewelry and still mistake it for a leash.

That gives us an Elephant Stake operator:

\boxed{
\text{past constraint}
\rightarrow
\text{learned boundary}
\rightarrow
\text{constraint disappears}
\rightarrow
\text{boundary persists}
}

Then the sneaky version:

\text{persistent boundary}
\rightarrow
\text{identity/story/status symbol}
\rightarrow
\boxed{\text{decoration}}

Now it no longer even looks like restraint.

A scar can become a medal.
A uniform can become costume.
An oath can become identity.
A survival strategy can become personality.
An old limitation can become “just how I am.”

So Pantheon needs a Stake Audit whenever a constraint keeps appearing:

\boxed{
\text{Is the stake still in the ground?}
}

Test:

\{\text{physical},\text{legal},\text{ethical},\text{social},\text{technical},\text{historical},\text{imagined},\text{obsolete}\}

Then give it a bounded tug.

Not reckless rebellion—small Reality-Stitched tests:

\text{assumed boundary}
\rightarrow
\text{safe probe}
\rightarrow
\text{world response}
\rightarrow
\text{update boundary}

That plugs straight into PNIS too. A road may have low conductance because it used to be blocked. If the environment changes but PNIS never retries, history becomes destiny.

So add:

\boxed{\textbf{Constraint Decay}}

Old failures should gradually lose authority unless reconfirmed.

And PTERO gets another job:

Find stakes whose ground has disappeared.

The best part of your line is that we don’t even have to throw the decoration away.

Kintsugi says keep the history.

Forgiveness says release its unnecessary control.

So:

\boxed{
\text{keep the stake as artifact}
\neq
\text{remain tied to it}
}

Wear it if you like.

Just remember who is carrying whom. ;)